{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md", "content": "# Distinguish visual heterogeneity from task-rule drift

A fresh stream used demos with very different dimensions, palettes, backgrounds, and motifs from the test. The demos' observed edits were themselves disparate localized edits: demo 1 changed a pair of 5s to 6s; demo 2 changed a single 8 to 9; demo 3 changed a 7-cell to 9; demo 4 changed a local 7 region to 8; demo 5 changed two 7s to 3; demo 6 recolored a compact 2x3 patch from 2 to 5. No shared selector or abstract transformation was derived, and repeated requests were made without analyzing a cumulative delta ledger. The session's final candidate merely recolored the test's 8 structures to 4 without support from the demos.

Reusable inference lesson: neither assume that different-looking demos are unrelated nor force a common rule based on colors/salience. Precisely record each pair's delta, object context, and unchanged candidates; then seek measurable invariant relations (shape, relative position, local neighborhood, object role) across examples. Test any proposed rule by exact full-output replay on every demo. If deltas remain unlinked, further demos require a specific competing-hypothesis prediction; serial requests do not resolve uncertainty by themselves. For the test, keep its actual colors and structures unchanged unless a validated rule predicts edits.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md", "content": "# Separating markers from a stamp template

For a grid with scattered single-color marks and a distinctive multicolor shape, map the output delta against the input before deciding the shape's role. Record each copied footprint's exact coordinates and colors, then compare those to both scattered markers and any instance embedded in the source template. The template's own center may or may not be an active marker; infer this from the demo, rather than assuming all similar-colored cells are equivalent.

Define one explicit anchor and offsets for every template cell, including vertical/asymmetric parts. Enumerate marker positions from the original input before applying any edits. Replay on each demo and compare the full output, including unchanged cells, overlap, clipping, and whether the source template persists. A visually plausible generic cross is not a substitute for the observed exact template.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-incorrect-submission-recovery", "file": "references/path-selection-and-paint.md", "content": "# Separating path selection from recoloring

Straight-line or ray-filling tasks require two distinct inferences: (1) which endpoints/path cells are selected, and (2) which cells on that path change color. Do not assume that every aligned marker pair is connected, that only orthogonal or all eight directions are used, or that every intermediate cell is recolored. For each demonstration, record candidate alignments, intervening cells, old→new values, and aligned candidates that stayed unchanged. Then replay the full rule on immutable input copies and compare every output cell. A wrong result rejects the combined rule; revise endpoint selection and paint behavior as separate hypotheses rather than making cosmetic adjustments to the rendered grid.

Useful test matrix: endpoint color/identity; nearest versus any collinear partner; direction set; intervening-marker blocking; path termination; intermediate cells eligible by color; endpoint preservation; path overlaps. A candidate is ready only when these choices exactly reproduce all demos.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-incorrect-submission-recovery/references/path-selection-and-paint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/task-20746363-review.md", "content": "# Session review: compact-output task with varied demonstrations

This session had a test grid partitioned by separator rows/columns, while the demonstrations varied widely in size, palette, and output dimensions. The solver did not establish a consistent tile-to-output correspondence or exact encoding from any demo. It guessed constant/compact patterns, submitted a repeated 4x2 candidate after it was rejected, and requested several more demonstrations without adequately integrating their evidence. Those actions produced incorrect answers and unnecessary cost.

Reusable lesson: for compact partitioned outputs, explicitly enumerate panels, separators, output shape, marker coordinates/counts, and output values for every demo. Keep dimensions, correspondence, selector, and encoding as independent hypotheses. A changed palette/scale across demos may still admit an abstract shared mapping, but do not infer one from appearance. Replay any proposed rule exactly across every complete demo before submitting. After a wrong submission, discard the entire failed rule; never repeat the same grid. Request a demo only when two explicit live hypotheses predict different, answer-relevant results. Maintain the Continual-ARC contract: exactly one legal JSON action on the final line on every turn.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/task-20746363-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/compact-output-reassessment-after-errors.md", "content": "# Compact-output reassessment after incorrect submissions

When inputs are partitioned by repeated separator rows or columns but demos produce much smaller outputs, dimension ratios help identify possible structure but do not establish the encoding. Keep distinct hypotheses for (a) summarizing interior regions, (b) recording separator/boundary markers at their intersections, and (c) selecting only rows or columns with special markers. For each demo, write down the exact partition counts and map candidate input coordinates or blocks to each output cell, including ordering. Reproduce every output exactly before transferring the map.

After an incorrect compact-output submission, do not merely alter colors or copy another visually salient set of markers. Reopen the task class and output representation, and re-derive dimensions and correspondence from all demos. Repeated, heterogeneous demos may share only an abstract structure; spend another demo only when two explicit mappings predict a materially different discriminating observation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/compact-output-reassessment-after-errors.md"}
